    {
      "page_title": "Why mid = low + (high - low) / 2",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "low and high are plain `int` indices",
            "Their **sum** can exceed the int limit",
            "The sum silently ==wraps== to a negative",
            "mid then points outside the array"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Both forms give the ==same midpoint==. Only the safe one never builds a value larger than `high`."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "2. Integer Limits",
          "items": [
            "`int` max = ==2,147,483,647==",
            "`long` max ≈ 9.2 x 10^18",
            "Java / C++ **wrap**, they do not warn"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "lines": [
            "int low  = 1000000000;",
            "int high = 2000000000;",
            "int mid = (low + high) / 2;",
            "// sum = 3e9 > int max",
            "// wraps to -1294967296",
            "// mid = -647483648 (bad!)"
          ],
          "note": "sum overflows before the divide"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "3. Expression",
            "Value",
            "Fits int?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "low + high",
              "3,000,000,000",
              "**No**, wraps"
            ],
            [
              "(low+high)/2",
              "-647,483,648",
              "wrong index"
            ],
            [
              "high - low",
              "1,000,000,000",
              "Yes"
            ],
            [
              "low + (high-low)/2",
              "1,500,000,000",
              "==Yes=="
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The Safe Formula",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "lines": [
            "int gap = high - low; // >= 0",
            "int mid = low + gap / 2;",
            "// gap <= high, never overflows",
            "// low + gap/2 <= high, safe"
          ],
          "note": "never builds the huge sum"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "low + high",
                "sub": "3,000,000,000",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "exceeds int max",
                "sub": "2,147,483,647",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "wraps negative",
                "sub": "-1,294,967,296",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "mid is negative",
                "sub": "crash or wrong half",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. The >>> Shift Variant",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "lines": [
            "int mid = (low + high) >>> 1;",
            "// the wrapped sum still",
            "// holds the right 32 bits",
            "// >>> shifts in a zero"
          ],
          "note": "Java's own fix in Arrays"
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "The overflow-safe midpoint"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The >>> Variant & When to Go long",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The wrap keeps every ==bit==, only the sign lies",
            "`>>>` re-reads those bits as unsigned, then halves",
            "Valid only while low and high are **non-negative**",
            "C / C++ have no `>>>`: cast to `unsigned` instead"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Write the safe form **always**, by habit",
            "Interviewers look for this one line",
            "Costs nothing: same O(1) work",
            "Use `long` when bounds are still bigger"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Same Midpoint, Proved",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "low + (high - low) / 2",
            "= (2·low + high - low) / 2",
            "= (low + high) / 2, the ==same== point",
            "Both floor alike when 0 <= low <= high"
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "7. Where It Bites",
          "items": [
            "Arrays over ==1 billion== elements",
            "BS on **answer** with huge ranges",
            "high = sum(arr) in Allocate Books",
            "Hash / prefix sums used as bounds"
          ],
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "lines": [
            "long low = maxWeight;",
            "long high = sumOfWeights;",
            "long mid = low+(high-low)/2;",
            "// sum(arr) blows past int",
            "// keep the whole range long"
          ],
          "note": "widen bounds, not just mid"
        },
        {
